const kg1=[
 ['My name and my classroom','Introduce oneself and recognize a personal name card.','Large-print name cards, crayons, classroom picture cards','Model a greeting and introduce yourself in the familiar classroom language.','Invite children to choose their own name card from two options, greet a partner and draw a self-portrait. Count the portraits in a small group and take turns describing one classroom object.','Invite each child to identify their card and greet a puppet.','Accept a spoken introduction, gesture or communication aid; recognizing a familiar card is not proof of independent reading.'],
 ['My body and safe movement','Identify familiar body parts and follow a simple movement instruction.','Body outline poster, paper, crayons','Sing an action song using hands and feet; offer seated actions too.','Point to hands, feet, head and shoulders on a picture. Children mirror one safe action, draw themselves and count two hands. Discuss asking permission before touching another person.','Ask children to point to two named body parts and show one safe action.','Look for accurate identification and controlled movement; support participation without requiring children to expose or discuss private body parts.'],
 ['My senses','Describe an object using a safe sense observation.','Large smooth and textured objects, picture cards','Show a textured cloth and describe what you notice by looking.','Set up looking and supervised touch stations with large clean objects. Children describe colour or texture, sort two textures and draw an object. Do not use tasting or blindfolds; provide a visual alternative for touch.','Offer two objects and ask the child to describe one difference.','Accept relevant differences such as smooth/rough or red/blue; distinguish an observation from a guess.'],
 ['Keeping clean','Sequence a handwashing routine and explain when it is useful.','Water, soap, basin, picture sequence cards','Show clean and dirty-hand pictures and ask when hands need washing.','Model wetting hands, applying soap, rubbing all surfaces, rinsing and drying. Children order picture cards and practise with adult supervision. Use a short counting chant while rubbing and draw a soap-and-water reminder.','Ask each child to arrange three simplified routine pictures and name a time to wash.','Observe soap use, rubbing and rinsing; before eating and after using the toilet are suitable examples. Avoid sharing dirty water or towels.'],
 ['People who care for us','Describe a caring action and recognize that families differ.','Inclusive caregiver picture cards, drawing paper','Tell a short story about someone helping a child get ready for school.','Children choose pictures showing caring actions, role-play offering help and draw someone who cares for them. Count people in invented picture families, without ranking family size or requiring personal disclosure.','Ask the child to name or show a helpful action in a picture.','Feeding, comforting or helping someone safely are acceptable; do not assume all children live with parents.'],
 ['Foods we know','Name familiar foods and sort pictures using an explained feature.','Food pictures, baskets, crayons','Invite children to name a familiar food in their home language.','Discuss pictures of fruits, vegetables and prepared meals. Children group pictures by colour, count up to five items and draw a pretend meal. Model polite requests in pretend play. Use pictures rather than shared tasting.','Give three pictures and ask the child to make and explain a grouping.','Accept a consistent criterion; do not label a child’s family food choices as good or bad.'],
 ['Our clothes','Describe clothing and choose an item for rainy or sunny weather.','Clean dress-up items or pictures, weather cards','Show a rain picture and ask what could help keep someone dry.','Pairs match clothing pictures to weather, describe colours and count buttons on a large picture. Create a paper clothing collage and practise asking permission before borrowing an item.','Ask the child to choose clothing for a rainy journey and explain.','A raincoat or umbrella is a reasonable choice; accept local practical alternatives and avoid gender-based clothing rules.'],
 ['Homes and familiar shapes','Recognize circles, triangles and rectangles in pictures of homes.','House pictures, large shape cut-outs, glue','Point to a pictured window and ask what its outline looks like.','Children build a picture home using shapes, name or match shapes and tell a partner about an imagined room. Count chosen shapes up to five. Discuss that homes can look different.','Ask each child to match a triangle and a circle and describe their picture.','A circle has a curved outline; a triangle has three sides. Accept pointing instead of verbal naming.'],
 ['Helping at home','Follow a one-step instruction in a safe helping task.','Large toy objects, baskets, picture task cards','Model putting one toy into a basket after play.','Role-play tidying safe classroom materials. Children sort objects by type, count a small group and describe a helpful action. Make a picture reminder. Explain that hot, sharp or electrical tasks need an adult.','Give a one-step instruction such as put the blocks in the basket.','Observe understanding and safe handling rather than speed; no lifting heavy items or handling chemicals.'],
 ['Kind words and turn-taking','Use a polite request and take a supported turn.','Puppet, large shared drawing tools, turn card','Have a puppet ask for a crayon politely.','Pairs practise requesting a tool, responding and passing it. Tell a short story about sharing, identify feelings in pictures and count turns with large cards. Allow a communication board or gestures.','Ask the child to show how to request a turn in a role-play.','Look for a respectful request and a response; children may need adult help to regulate waiting.'],
 ['Colours and repeating patterns','Copy a two-part repeating pattern.','Large coloured blocks or paper shapes','Arrange red-blue-red-blue blocks and ask what comes next.','Children copy an AB pattern with large shapes, say or point to its repeating unit and create a matching movement pattern. Draw a short pattern and explain it to a partner.','Show yellow-green-yellow-green and ask the child to choose the next two pieces.','Expected yellow then green; accept any consistent two-part pattern the child creates.'],
 ['My learning celebration','Demonstrate and describe selected learning through play.','Name cards, shape pictures, pattern pieces, handwashing sequence cards','Revisit a familiar song and invite a child-selected action.','Set up short supported stations for name-card recognition, shapes, patterns and a safe-care routine. Observe each child individually before group play. Invite drawings about a favourite activity and plan follow-up from observations.','Ask for one name-card choice, one shape match and one copied AB pattern.','Record each skill separately; use observations to plan support, not to rank children or demand written examinations.'],
];
const kg2=[
 ['My identity and belonging','Introduce oneself using a short sentence and identify a personal name.','Name cards, drawing paper, crayons','Model a full-sentence introduction with your preferred name.','Children identify their name among several cards, draw a self-portrait and describe a strength. Count portrait features and make a class display with consent. Compare letter shapes without treating copied names as fluent reading.','Invite a sentence introduction and a name-card choice.','Accept a communication aid or familiar language; check whether the child distinguishes their card without location clues.'],
 ['Body care and healthy routines','Explain a simple body-care routine in sequence.','Routine picture cards, dolls, soap-and-water pictures','Ask a puppet what it does before eating.','Children sequence washing hands, eating and cleaning up, act out a safe routine and draw a reminder. Count the picture steps and describe first/next/last. Practise handwashing under adult supervision.','Ask children to order three routine cards and explain one reason for washing.','Washing before eating reduces dirt and germs; model the routine without promising that it prevents every illness.'],
 ['Senses and describing words','Compare objects using two observable properties.','Large textured shapes, observation cards','Describe a large object without naming it and invite guesses.','Pairs observe safe objects, name colour and texture, sort by a stated rule and draw two comparisons. Introduce describing words orally in familiar language and English where appropriate. No tasting, blindfolding or forced touch.','Ask for two properties of an object or a matching picture choice.','Relevant properties include shape, colour and texture; provide visual choices for children who avoid touch.'],
 ['Expressing feelings and seeking help','Name a feeling and practise seeking help from a trusted adult.','Feeling-face cards, puppet, drawing paper','Tell a fictional story about a child who cannot find a classroom item.','Children choose a face card, practise saying I need help and role-play approaching the class teacher. Draw a comforting activity and count faces in a picture. Do not ask children to disclose distressing personal events.','Ask the child to show how the fictional character could ask for help.','Accept gestures and communication aids; never promise secrecy about safety concerns.'],
 ['Families and shared responsibilities','Describe respectful ways people help one another.','Inclusive family pictures, large counters','Compare two fictional families without judging their size or structure.','Small groups act out sharing a safe task, count people in picture families up to ten and tell a short story about cooperation. Draw a helper and label with a dictated word if desired.','Ask for a caring action and a count from a six-person picture.','Helping or comforting is suitable; an accurate six-person count requires one count per person.'],
 ['A pretend food market','Use oral requests and count items in pretend exchange.','Food picture cards, large pretend tokens, baskets','Model asking a seller for three mango pictures.','Set up a pretend market with picture goods costing one token each. Children request up to ten items, exchange equal tokens and explain their choice. Draw a pretend shopping list; avoid real-food allergy and hygiene issues.','Ask for four items and the matching number of tokens.','Expected four items and four tokens; check one-to-one correspondence rather than memorized counting.'],
 ['Clothing, weather and patterns','Choose practical clothing and extend a three-part pattern.','Weather pictures, large coloured shapes, paper clothing outlines','Show rainy and sunny weather pictures and invite clothing suggestions.','Children match clothing to weather, design a paper garment with a red-blue-yellow repeating border and describe their pattern. Count pieces in one repeating unit and compare designs.','Ask the child to continue red-blue-yellow-red-blue with two pieces.','Expected yellow then red; practical clothing choices may vary by local conditions.'],
 ['Homes and building shapes','Combine and describe flat shapes in a construction picture.','Large circles, triangles, rectangles and squares','Compare a square window picture and a circular window picture.','Children build an imagined home from flat shapes, count up to ten pieces and explain where a triangle or rectangle was used. Partners ask questions and add a drawing. Distinguish a flat picture from a solid object.','Ask the child to identify a triangle and count its sides.','A triangle has three straight sides; do not require a fixed orientation to recognize it.'],
 ['Our classroom community','Follow a two-step instruction and explain a helpful classroom rule.','Task pictures, baskets, large classroom objects','Model a two-step instruction and ask learners to repeat or show it.','Pairs practise putting large blocks in a basket then placing a picture label beside it. Discuss why clear walkways help everyone. Draw a classroom-rule poster and sort pictures into safe and unsafe choices.','Ask the child to fetch a picture card then put it beside a basket.','Record each step independently; repeat or break down the instruction when needed without labelling the learner careless.'],
 ['Stories about caring','Retell a short story with a beginning, middle and end.','Three original story picture cards, puppet','Tell a short invented story about returning a lost classroom crayon.','Children order three pictures, retell the story in a familiar language and role-play a kind response. Count characters and draw a new ending. Encourage different sensible endings rather than one memorized script.','Ask the child to order the pictures and explain what happened next.','Look for a coherent sequence; support with who/what/next prompts and accept oral or gestural retelling.'],
 ['Counting, combining and sharing','Combine small groups and share a small collection equally.','Large counters, two plates, number cards','Show two counters and add one, counting the total together.','Children combine groups totalling up to ten, then share six large counters equally between two pretend characters. Draw the groups and tell a number story. Use large materials under supervision; offer a pictorial alternative.','Ask the child to combine three and two, then share six into two equal groups.','Expected five in total and three in each group; check both equal size and whether every counter was shared.'],
 ['Our learning story','Demonstrate integrated language, number and cooperation skills.','Story cards, large counters, shapes, drawing materials','Ask children to choose a favourite classroom activity and explain why.','Rotate through a story-retelling station, a number-sharing station and a cooperative shape picture. Observe before offering prompts. Children draw one learning achievement and dictate a sentence; plan the next steps from the observations.','Ask for a three-picture retelling, a count of eight and an equal share of eight between two.','Eight shared between two gives four each. Record language, number and participation separately and plan supportive follow-up.'],
];
